<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Namara inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/328">328</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Namara inscription is an Old Arabic text carved in Nabataean script on a tomb lintel at al-Namara in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and dated to 328 CE. It commemorates Imru al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>, son of Amr, as king of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and is usually linked to the early Lakhmid rulers of al-Hira. Found in 1901, it is one of the earliest substantial examples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a> and is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/namara-inscription.jpg" alt="Namara inscription" width="320" height="113" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epitaph_Imru-l-Qays_Louvre_AO4083.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The inscription is carved in five lines on a block of basalt that may have been the lintel of a tomb. Its subject, Imru' al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> ibn 'Amr, followed his father 'Amr ibn Adi in conquering much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> from al-Hirah as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Sassanids. When raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> provoked a campaign by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>, he retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to seek help from the Roman emperor Constantine. Hisham Ibn Al-Kalbi, an early ninth-century scholar, says he became a Christian before his death, though Irfan Shahîd notes that the inscription has not a single Christian formula or symbol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In James A. Bellamy's widely accepted translation, the text names the monument as that of Imru' al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>, son of 'Amr, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and lists his deeds: he subdued the Asadis and their kings, put Madhhij to flight and drove them to the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a>, the city of Shammar, subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maadd">Ma'add</a>, and made the nobles of the tribes viceroys, who became phylarchs for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. It closes with the date of his death, the 7th day of Kaslul in the year 223 of the Nabatean era of Bostra, which corresponds to 7 December 328.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>René Dussaud and Frédéric Macler found the stone on 4 April 1901 at al-Namara, about 100 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, near the Roman frontier of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-petraea">Arabia Petraea</a>. Dussaud published the first tracing and reading, and later scholars, while differing in detail, kept his view that it was the king's burial monument. In 1985 Bellamy corrected the tracing of two contested words in the third line. The script has also been read as a late form of Nabataean in transition to Arabic. Irfan Shahid called it the most important Arabic inscription of pre-Islamic times; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> holds it as AO 4083.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Namara inscription” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The most famous early text is the <b>Namara inscription</b> of 328 CE, found in southern Syria, the epitaph of Imru' al-Qays, who is called “king of all the Arabs”, written in Arabic in Nabataean letters.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Namara inscription</b> of 328 commemorates Imru al-Qays, son of Amr, as king of all the Arabs; he is usually counted as an early Lakhmid ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/lakhmid-kingdom">Lakhmid Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
